Title: "Holomorphic Functions and Cauchy-Riemann"
Id: "42e6f00e-b0dc-4e31-8070-31f87aaacb8b"


Text: "The transition from real multivariable calculus to complex analysis is marked by an
       extraordinary tightening of differentiability. In multivariable calculus on $\mathbb{R}^2$,
       a mapping $F(x, y) = (u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is differentiable at $(x_0, y_0)$ if its increments
       are linearly approximated by a total derivative represented by an arbitrary $2 \times 2$
       real Jacobian matrix:
       $$ DF(x_0, y_0) = \begin{pmatrix} u_x & u_y \\ v_x & v_y \end{pmatrix}. $$
       Such a linear operator has four independent degrees of freedom; it can stretch different
       directions by different ratios and shear angles between intersecting curves.

       In complex analysis, by contrast, a function $f: U \to \mathbb{C}$ is complex differentiable
       if the limit of the difference quotient
       $$ f'(z_0) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0)}{h} $$
       exists as a single complex number $a + ib$. Because multiplication by $a + ib$ corresponds
       in $\mathbb{R}^2$ to the rotation-dilation matrix
       $$ \begin{pmatrix} a & -b \\ b & a \end{pmatrix}, $$
       the requirement of complex differentiability constrains the four entries of the Jacobian matrix
       to satisfy $u_x = v_y = a$ and $u_y = -v_x = -b$. These are the celebrated *Cauchy--Riemann equations*.
       This single geometric constraint forces $DF(x_0, y_0)$ to be an orientation-preserving similarity,
       preserving all angles and scaling all infinitesimal tangent directions equally. From this local
       isotropy springs the entire majestic rigidity of holomorphic functions."
Id: "f9443fcc-51df-4c3e-aae1-18af70fc23f0"


SectionTitle: "Complex Differentiability and Holomorphy"
Id: "713f738e-b14f-4cfe-9c0c-a92e1a0ea642"


TextDefinition: "Let $U \subseteq \mathbb{C}$ be an open set and let $f: U \to \mathbb{C}$. The function
                 $f$ is *complex differentiable* at a point $z_0 \in U$ if there exists a complex number
                 $f'(z_0) \in \mathbb{C}$ such that for every $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists $\delta > 0$
                 such that for all $h \in \mathbb{C}$ with $0 < |h| < \delta$,
                 $$ \left| \frac{f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0)}{h} - f'(z_0) \right| < \varepsilon. $$
                 Equivalently, $f$ admits the first-order Taylor expansion:
                 $$ f(z_0 + h) = f(z_0) + f'(z_0) h + \eta(h) h, \quad \text{where } \lim_{h \to 0} \eta(h) = 0. $$
                 The function $f$ is said to be *holomorphic* (or *analytic*) on $U$ if it is complex
                 differentiable at every point of $U$. If $f$ is holomorphic on the entire complex plane
                 $\mathbb{C}$, it is called *entire*."
Id: "d72a037b-3028-4c74-a2e8-3dd5e96522a7"


[\complex.derivative:of{f}:at{z0}]
Defines: d is \complex
when:
. z0 is \\anything
. f is \\anything
expresses:
. forAll: epsilon is \real
  where: epsilon \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
  then:
  . exists: delta is \real
    suchThat:
    . delta \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
    . forAll: h is \complex
      where:
      . h != \complex.zero
      . \complex.modulus(h) \:real.less?:/ delta
      then: \complex.modulus(((f(z0 \.complex.+./ h) \.complex.-./ f(z0)) \.complex.div./ h) \.complex.-./ d) \:real.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. called: "complex derivative of $f?$ at $z0?$"
. written: "f?'(z0?)"
. description: "The complex derivative
                $f'(z_0) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0)}{h}$."
Id: "7e3019a7-07f3-462e-bd30-6047f158b5de"


[\(holomorphic)::complex.function:on{U}]
Refines: f
when: U \:subset:/ \complex.numbers
specifies: f is \function:on{U}:to{\complex.numbers}
satisfies:
. forAll: z0 "in" U
  then:
  . exists: d is \complex
    suchThat: d = \complex.derivative:of{f}:at{z0}
Documented:
. adjective: "holomorphic"
. description: "A function is holomorphic on $U$ if it is complex-differentiable
                at every point in $U$."
Id: "f2a708e1-6ee2-4072-aa11-08f5e90fe31f"


SectionTitle: "The Cauchy-Riemann System and Harmonicity"
Id: "ad8cf71b-7b92-4811-8375-694f86854f97"


TextTheorem: "The Cauchy--Riemann Equations. Let $f(x + iy) = u(x, y) + i v(x, y)$ be defined on an open
              set $U \subseteq \mathbb{C}$ with real-valued component functions $u, v: U \to \mathbb{R}$.
              
              1. **Necessity**: If $f$ is complex differentiable at $z_0 = x_0 + i y_0$, then the first-order
                 partial derivatives of $u$ and $v$ exist at $(x_0, y_0)$ and satisfy the Cauchy--Riemann equations:
                 $$ \frac{\partial u}{\partial x} = \frac{\partial v}{\partial y} \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} = -\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}. $$
                 Furthermore, the complex derivative may be computed via either partial derivative:
                 $$ f'(z_0) = u_x(x_0, y_0) + i v_x(x_0, y_0) = v_y(x_0, y_0) - i u_y(x_0, y_0). $$

              2. **Sufficiency**: Conversely, if the real partial derivatives $u_x, u_y, v_x, v_y$ exist on an
                 open neighborhood of $(x_0, y_0)$, are continuous at $(x_0, y_0)$ (so that $u$ and $v$ are
                 $C^1$ differentiable in the real sense), and satisfy the Cauchy--Riemann equations at $(x_0, y_0)$,
                 then $f$ is complex differentiable at $z_0$.

              *Proof of Necessity*: Compute the difference quotient along two orthogonal directions. First, let
              $h = \Delta x \in \mathbb{R}$ approach $0$ along the horizontal real axis:
              $$ f'(z_0) = \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{u(x_0 + \Delta x, y_0) - u(x_0, y_0)}{\Delta x} + i \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{v(x_0 + \Delta x, y_0) - v(x_0, y_0)}{\Delta x} = u_x(x_0, y_0) + i v_x(x_0, y_0). $$
              Second, let $h = i \Delta y$ with $\Delta y \in \mathbb{R}$ approach $0$ along the vertical imaginary axis:
              $$ f'(z_0) = \lim_{\Delta y \to 0} \frac{u(x_0, y_0 + \Delta y) - u(x_0, y_0)}{i \Delta y} + i \lim_{\Delta y \to 0} \frac{v(x_0, y_0 + \Delta y) - v(x_0, y_0)}{i \Delta y} = \frac{u_y + i v_y}{i} = v_y(x_0, y_0) - i u_y(x_0, y_0). $$
              Equating the real and imaginary parts of these two expressions yields $u_x = v_y$ and $v_x = -u_y$.
              
              *Proof of Sufficiency*: If $u$ and $v$ are $C^1$, the multivariable mean value theorem guarantees:
              $$ \Delta u = u_x \Delta x + u_y \Delta y + \varepsilon_1 \sqrt{\Delta x^2 + \Delta y^2}, \quad \Delta v = v_x \Delta x + v_y \Delta y + \varepsilon_2 \sqrt{\Delta x^2 + \Delta y^2}, $$
              where $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2 \to 0$ as $h = \Delta x + i \Delta y \to 0$. Substituting $u_y = -v_x$ and $v_y = u_x$:
              $$ \Delta u + i \Delta v = (u_x \Delta x - v_x \Delta y) + i(v_x \Delta x + u_x \Delta y) + o(|h|) = (u_x + i v_x)(\Delta x + i \Delta y) + o(|h|) = (u_x + i v_x) h + o(|h|). $$
              Dividing by $h \ne 0$ and taking the limit $h \to 0$ proves that $f'(z_0) = u_x + i v_x$ exists."
Id: "da109af8-129c-4931-86b0-9dfbc1b146d3"


TextDefinition: "Let $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be an open set. A twice continuously differentiable function
                 $u: U \to \mathbb{R}$ is *harmonic* on $U$ if it satisfies Laplace's partial differential
                 equation throughout $U$:
                 $$ \Delta u = \nabla^2 u = \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} + \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial y^2} = 0. $$
                 If $f = u + iv$ is holomorphic on $U$, then both its real part $u$ and imaginary part $v$ are
                 harmonic. The function $v$ is called a *harmonic conjugate* of $u$.
                 
                 *Derivation*: Differentiating the Cauchy--Riemann equations and applying Clairaut's theorem
                 on the equality of mixed partial derivatives ($u_{xy} = u_{yx}$ and $v_{xy} = v_{yx}$):
                 $$ u_{xx} = (u_x)_x = (v_y)_x = v_{yx} = v_{xy} = (-u_y)_y = -u_{yy} \implies u_{xx} + u_{yy} = 0. $$
                 Furthermore, the gradients $\nabla u = (u_x, u_y)$ and $\nabla v = (v_x, v_y) = (-u_y, u_x)$
                 satisfy $\nabla u \cdot \nabla v = -u_x u_y + u_y u_x = 0$. Consequently, the level curves
                 $u(x,y) = c_1$ and $v(x,y) = c_2$ form two mutually orthogonal families of curves throughout $U$."
Id: "0dbcf796-c093-475e-943c-203eaeee9037"


Text: "The continuity of partial derivatives in the converse of the
       Cauchy--Riemann theorem is essential. The mere existence of directional
       derivatives along the coordinate axes satisfying $u_x = v_y$ and
       $u_y = -v_x$ at an isolated point does not guarantee complex
       differentiability. A classic counterexample is given by
       $f(z) = \sqrt{|xy|}$ at the origin: both partial derivatives vanish at
       $(0,0)$, trivially satisfying the Cauchy--Riemann equations, yet $f$
       fails to be complex differentiable because the difference quotient along
       diagonal directions $z = t(1+i)$ does not converge to $0$.

       The deep *Looman--Menchoff Theorem* establishes the ultimate boundary of
       this phenomenon: if $f$ is continuous on an open domain $U$ and its
       partial derivatives $u_x, u_y, v_x, v_y$ exist everywhere on $U$ and
       satisfy the Cauchy--Riemann equations almost everywhere, then $f$ is
       holomorphic on $U$."
Id: "6e2e106e-d0bc-41a1-87e9-4a6a54d21afe"


TextTheorem: "The Open Mapping Theorem and Maximum Modulus Principle.

              1. **Open Mapping Theorem**: If $U \subseteq \mathbb{C}$ is a
                 connected open domain and $f: U \to \mathbb{C}$ is a
                 nonconstant holomorphic function, then $f(U)$ is an open subset
                 of $\mathbb{C}$. That is, $f$ maps open sets to open sets.

              2. **Maximum Modulus Principle**: Let $U \subseteq \mathbb{C}$ be
                 a bounded connected domain and let
                 $f: \overline{U} \to \mathbb{C}$ be continuous on the closure
                 $\overline{U}$ and holomorphic on $U$. Then the modulus
                 $|f(z)|$ attains its maximum on the boundary $\partial U$:
                 $$ \sup_{z \in U} |f(z)| \le \max_{z \in \partial U} |f(z)|. $$
                 Moreover, if $|f(z)|$ attains a local maximum at any interior
                 point $z_0 \in U$, then $f$ is identically constant on $U$.

              *Proof of Maximum Modulus Principle*: Suppose for contradiction
              that $|f|$ attains an interior local maximum at $z_0 \in U$, so
              $|f(z)| \le |f(z_0)|$ for all $z$ in some disk
              $D(z_0, r) \subseteq U$. If $f$ were nonconstant, the Open Mapping
              Theorem implies that the image $f(D(z_0, r))$ is an open
              neighborhood of $w_0 = f(z_0)$. But an open disk around $w_0$
              necessarily contains points $w$ with $|w| > |w_0|$, contradicting
              the assumption that $|f(z_0)|$ was a local maximum. Thus $f$ must
              be constant."
Id: "3a14cb79-f953-4f10-a4ec-5fd951ace89f"


TextTheorem: "Conformality and Complex Derivatives. Let $f: U \to \mathbb{C}$ be holomorphic on an open set $U$,
              and let $z_0 \in U$ be a point where $f'(z_0) \ne 0$.
              
              Then $f$ is *conformal* at $z_0$: it preserves both the magnitude and orientation of angles between
              smooth curves intersecting at $z_0$. Moreover, it scales all infinitesimal tangent vectors by the
              uniform factor $|f'(z_0)| > 0$.
              
              *Geometric Explanation*: Let $\gamma_1, \gamma_2: (-\varepsilon, \varepsilon) \to U$ be smooth curves
              with $\gamma_1(0) = \gamma_2(0) = z_0$ and tangent vectors $v_1 = \gamma_1'(0)$, $v_2 = \gamma_2'(0)$.
              The tangent vectors to the image curves $(f \circ \gamma_1)$ and $(f \circ \gamma_2)$ at $f(z_0)$ are:
              $$ (f \circ \gamma_j)'(0) = f'(z_0) \gamma_j'(0) = f'(z_0) v_j. $$
              Writing $f'(z_0) = R e^{i\alpha}$ with $R = |f'(z_0)| > 0$ and $\alpha = \arg(f'(z_0))$, each tangent
              vector is rotated by the identical angle $\alpha$ and multiplied by the scalar $R$. Therefore, the angle
              between the image tangent vectors is:
              $$ \arg(f'(z_0) v_2) - \arg(f'(z_0) v_1) = (\alpha + \arg v_2) - (\alpha + \arg v_1) = \arg v_2 - \arg v_1. $$
              If $f'(z_0) = 0$ (a *critical point*), conformality fails: if $f(z) - f(z_0) = a_k (z - z_0)^k + O((z-z_0)^{k+1})$
              with $a_k \ne 0$ ($k \ge 2$), then angles between curves at $z_0$ are multiplied by $k$, as seen
              in $f(z) = z^k$ where the right angle between coordinate axes is mapped to an angle of $k \pi / 2$."
Id: "c4a53239-7a55-42f6-b469-e71554a3d2f9"
